          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"“Meat” primarily signifies advanced or substantial spiritual teaching suited to mature believers, contrasted with elementary “milk”; doing God’s will is a recognized secondary meaning.\",\"book_summary\":\"“Meat” is the word of God carried through into complete obedience, while “milk” is that same word merely received; maturity consists in obedient practice that exercises discernment.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Advanced spiritual teaching or doctrine for mature believers.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The enacted or completely obeyed word of God.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book rejects the usual content-level distinction between elementary and advanced teaching: milk and meat may be the same word, differentiated by reception versus completed obedience. It retains passages about maturity and God’s will but reclassifies them around obedient enactment rather than doctrinal depth.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"The central referent is replaced. Consensus identifies meat with the teaching received by mature believers; the book identifies it with obedience produced from the word and explicitly distinguishes this from merely receiving the word. Thus advanced teaching and its obedient enactment are not the same referent, even though they may be causally related.\",\"rationale\":\"The book’s reading is recognizable within the consensus alternative based on John 4:34—doing God’s will as spiritual nourishment—so it is not novel. Nevertheless, it elevates that alternative into the governing definition and uses it to reinterpret the milk/meat contrast. This materially displaces the consensus core rather than merely narrowing which advanced doctrines count as meat. Some extension overlaps remain, especially John 4 and Hebrews 5, but those cases are assigned a different symbolic basis.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"John 4:34 directly supports doing and finishing God’s will as Jesus’ meat, and Luke 8:21 and Hebrews 5:14 connect obedience or practiced use with maturity. However, the references do not clearly establish the precise universal equation “milk = the word received; meat = the same word obeyed completely.” Hebrews 5 naturally supports mature solid teaching, while Matthew 24:45 may depict meat as instruction supplied to others, and the key milk-context verses are not printed.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
